# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in the two values, which are on the Supabase
# dashboard under Settings → API. `.env.local` is gitignored; this file is committed.
#
# BOTH VALUES ARE PUBLIC BY DESIGN. They ship inside the client bundle — the `NEXT_PUBLIC_`
# prefix is what tells Next.js to inline them — and they are stored as GitHub repository
# *variables* rather than secrets for that reason. What protects the data is row-level
# security, not the secrecy of this key. Masking them in CI logs would buy nothing and make
# a failed build harder to read.
#
# THE SERVICE-ROLE KEY IS NOT HERE AND MUST NOT BE ADDED. It bypasses row-level security
# entirely. The one thing in this repository that writes to the database — the authenticated
# row-level security suite — does so as an ordinary signed-in member precisely so that the
# policies are exercised rather than skipped. A service-role key would make that suite pass
# unconditionally. It stays in the Supabase dashboard.

# The bare project origin — no path, no trailing `/rest/v1/`. supabase-js appends that
# itself, and a value with a path makes every query fail with PGRST125.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# The `anon` public key, not `service_role`. Decoded, its JWT claims read `"role": "anon"`.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Needed only by `npm run test:db`. The site never reads this.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# UNLIKE THE TWO ABOVE, THIS ONE IS A REAL SECRET. It is the password of the Supabase account
# `rls-fixture@example.com`, which tests/rls/authenticated.test.ts signs in as — the only way
# to exercise policies that resolve the caller's organization from a live session. It has no
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix so Next cannot inline it into a bundle, it is a GitHub *secret*
# rather than a variable, and it must not be pasted into an issue, a pull request, or a chat.
#
# The account belongs to the fixture organization, never to the live center: a member can
# write everything its organization owns, so this password's blast radius is deliberately two
# rows that read "FIXTURE — must never be visible". See supabase/fixtures/rls.sql, which also
# covers creating the account and its profile row.
SUPABASE_TEST_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Needed only by the admin's Publish button, in the deployed environment. Added by #75.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ALSO A REAL SECRET. A GitHub fine-grained personal access token with **Actions: read and
# write** on this repository and nothing else. Pressing Publish uses it to dispatch
# .github/workflows/publish-content.yml, which rebuilds production from the newest release
# tag with the content that was just published.
#
# Anyone holding it can start unlimited production builds, which costs build minutes and
# republishes the site — so it is set by the owner in NETLIFY's environment variables (that is
# where the admin actually runs), never committed, and never pasted into an issue or a chat.
# It is deliberately NOT a GitHub Actions secret: CI has no reason to publish content.
#
# No `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix, so Next cannot inline it into a browser bundle. It is read only by
# lib/admin/publish.ts, which is imported only from a server action.
#
# Leaving it unset is safe and is what a working copy should do: publishing then reports that
# the rebuild could not be started, and — importantly — says the edits are still published and
# nothing was lost. Local development has no business rebuilding production.
GITHUB_PUBLISH_TOKEN=
